  [1mCosts you:[0m a bigger engine that must interpret every key and define rule-evaluation order. Anything the engine doesn't
  know needs a [38;5;153mcustom: fn[39m escape hatch, which drags functions back in and leaves you with two ways to express a rule.
  Cross-field rules fit badly. I'd pick this only if you had a server defining form schemas — you don't.

  [1mApproach C: One validate function per form[0m

  [38;5;153mbindForm[39m takes a whole [38;5;153m(values) => ({ valid, errors })[39m function; the module ships only helper predicates.

  [1mBuys you:[0m the least framework possible, maximum flexibility, nothing to learn.
  [1mCosts you:[0m it under-delivers on the actual ask. Every form re-writes the per-field loop and error assembly by hand —
  and that loop is precisely the duplication you asked me to remove.

  ---

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It's nearly as small as C but removes the duplication C leaves behind, and B's one real advantage —
  serializability — buys nothing in a repo with no backend and no server-driven forms, while costing a meaningfully
  larger engine.

  One contract worth flagging now because it's the expensive-to-change part, independent of which approach you pick:
  [38;5;153mvalidate[39m returns [38;5;153m{ valid: boolean, errors: { fieldName: "message" } }[39m, [1mfirst error per field wins[0m (a form typically
  shows one message per input). Every form and every test will encode against that shape.
